  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kiribati Australia Health Support Program (KAHSP) has successfully leveraged its bilateral partnerships to influence national health efforts, securing support from the World Scabies Program (WSP) and Fred Hollows Foundation (FHF). By showcasing local needs and facilitating collaboration, KAHSP helped align Mass Drug Administration (MDA) for scabies and trachoma between the three programs. 
&#x200d;
The integrated approach has reduced finance and staffing costs,  minimised community burden while maximising community reach, and improved health outcomes. This was the first model for coordinated neglected tropical disease interventions, attracting national recognition. The initiative not only strengthened Australia&#x2019;s development impact but also demonstrated the value of strategic partnerships in driving sustainable, community focused health solutions.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Kiribati Australia Health Sector Program (KAHSP), the Ministry of Health and Medical Services successfully mobilised clinical outreach medical teams to serve remote outer islands, significantly improving access to essential health services. Prior to this initiative many communities faced long delays and barriers to accessing quality care due to their isolation.
&#x200d;
KAHSP&#x2019;s contribution enabled logistical coordination, provision of medical supplies and strengthened human resource capacity. The outreach teams, comprising doctors, nurses and public health officers delivered a range of services including dental, minor surgery, obstetric and gynaecology, ear/nose/throat, non-communicable disease screening, and health promotion. This approach not only addressed immediate health needs but also supported 26 local nurses to go through on-site training and mentoring.
&#x200d;
A total of 1,703 people were seen resulting in increased early detection of illnesses, streamlined patient referrals, and increased community trust in the health system. The program also fostered better data collection and strengthened referral linkages between central and peripheral facilities.
&#x200d;
This success demonstrates the importance of strategic partnerships in bridging service delivery gaps, reinforcing systems resilience and bringing health care to even the most remote parts of Kiribati. This integrated outreach approach is now an ongoing activity under KAHSP promoting health system strengthening.</narrative>
    </description>
